## 1. Key Diagnostic Techniques

### Step 1: Check Manifest Location

The static pod manifest path is defined in `/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml` under
`staticPodPath: /etc/kubernetes/manifests`.

> [!CAUTION]
> **Static Pod Backup Location**: Never store backup files (e.g. `*.bak`, `*.old`) inside
> `/etc/kubernetes/manifests/`. Kubelet reads every non-hidden file in that directory as a static pod manifest. Backups
> must always be stored outside `manifests/` (e.g. `/etc/kubernetes/kube-scheduler.yaml.bak`).

### Step 2: Inspect Control Plane Pod Status

```bash
kubectl get pods -n kube-system
```

### Step 3: Inspect Directly via Container Runtime (`crictl`)

When the control plane API server is down or a component fails before connecting, use `crictl` directly on the host:

```bash
sudo crictl ps -a | grep -E "scheduler|controller|apiserver"
sudo crictl logs <container-id>
```

### Step 4: Inspect Host System Logs

```bash
sudo journalctl -u kubelet -e --no-pager
```

